.TH CLOUDAUTH 1
.SH NAME
cloudauth \- take this machine's identity from the provider
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cloudauth
[
.B -v
]
[
.B -k
.I keyfile
]
(
.B -m
.I attribute
|
.B -u
.I url
|
.B -f
.I file
)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cloudauth
fetches a certificate and installs it as this machine's identity, so
that
.IR styxlisten (1)
and
.IR mount (1)
can authenticate without
.BR -A .
.PP
Inferno's authentication is mutual and certificate-based: each side
holds an
.B Authinfo
signed by a common signer, exchanges it, and verifies the other against
the signer's public key that its own certificate carries.  Neither side
has to have met the other, and neither has to reach an authentication
server while they are talking \(em which is what makes it usable on a
provider, where the instance that has just booted may be the only one
running.
.PP
That leaves only the question of how each machine got its certificate,
and the provider already answers it: an instance is created with
metadata that only that instance can read, served on a link-local
address that asks for no credential.
.IP
.EX
gcloud compute instances create NAME \e
	--metadata-from-file inferno-cert=alice.cert
# then, on the instance
cloudauth -m inferno-cert
.EE
.PP
The source is given by exactly one of
.TP
.BI -m " attribute"
An instance metadata attribute.  The request carries
.BR "Metadata-Flavor: Google" ,
which the service requires.
.TP
.BI -u " url"
Any URL, including a signed object-storage URL.
.TP
.BI -f " file"
A file, which may be one served by
.IR cloudfs (4).
.PP
.B -k
names the file to write; the default is
.BI /usr/ user /keyring/default
for the user in
.BR /dev/user .
.B -v
reports the URL fetched and the owner and signer of what arrived.
.SH "WHERE THE IDENTITY GOES ON A KERNEL"
A kernel's root is compiled into the image and is read-only, so
.B /usr/inferno/keyring
cannot be written until something writable is bound over it.  On an
instance that is
.IR memfs (4):
.IP
.EX
memfs /usr/inferno/keyring
cloudauth -m inferno-cert
.EE
.PP
The certificate then lives in memory and is gone at reboot, which is
the right shape for an instance: it takes its identity from the
provider every time it starts, so revoking it is a metadata change and
not a visit to the machine.
.SH "WHAT IS VERIFIED, AND WHAT IS TRUSTED"
What arrives is parsed as an
.B Authinfo
before it is installed, and written beside the target and moved into
place only if that succeeds.  A 404 page, an error document or a
truncated fetch is still bytes, and writing it over a working identity
would fail later at the next mount, a long way from the cause.
.PP
The certificate carries a private key, so it is exactly as secret as a
password, and putting it in instance metadata means the provider's
access control is what protects it.  That is the same trust the
instance already places in the provider to boot the right image, but it
is worth saying rather than assuming: anything that can read the
instance's metadata can be that instance.
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve a directory to authenticated callers only:
.IP
.EX
memfs /usr/inferno/keyring
cloudauth -m inferno-cert
styxlisten 'tcp!*!6666' export /n/metric
.EE
.PP
Mount it from another instance that has done the same:
.IP
.EX
mount tcp!other!6666 /n/host
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cloudauth.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxlisten (1),
.IR cloudfs (4),
.IR memfs (4),
.IR mkauthinfo (8),
.IR createsignerkey (8)
.SH BUGS
It installs a certificate; it does not create one.  The signer and the
certificates are made elsewhere with
.IR createsignerkey (8)
and
.IR mkauthinfo (8),
and getting them into instance metadata is the operator's job.
.PP
.B -m
is exercised only on a real instance: the metadata service lives at a
link-local address that does not exist on a development machine, so
.B -u
is what the local check uses.
